{"rule":"Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged. The text field holds, in at most 1800 characters, everything a reader would need to know of the story so far, plus one new event that moves the story forward. It is written for a human reader.","text":"At Vardnes, forty-seven-year-old Marit Solvang watched the tide swallow her mussel bucket at 13:09, fifty-one minutes before the predicted high water. The lighthouse keeper, Anders Holm, had left on March 2, leaving his cottage key with her. His logbook showed the tides drifting earlier since the previous July: first four minutes, then nine, then twenty-two. Now the sea had reached her shed doorstep, forcing her to move the outboard motor into the loft. The cottage barometer had stayed at 1003 for a week. A dead gull lay on the shingle beneath three living ones on the breakwater.

Marit's grandmother, Signe, had kept the tide table for forty years; Marit knew better than to dismiss what she was seeing. In a village of 212 people, the next dependable arrival was the ferry Kittiwake, which passed the point at 16:40 on Tuesdays and Fridays. Her brother Tomas, at the fish plant in Bergen, would not normally phone until Sunday.

Before dark, Marit took Anders's logbook and walked to his cottage. Inside, she found a tide chart pinned face inward above his desk. She turned it over. Anders had drawn a line through the increasingly early tides and extended it to a date three days away. Beside it, in his cramped handwriting, he had written: 'Signe saw this once before. Ask her what happened to the boats.' Signe had been dead for eleven years. Marit took the chart down; her grandmother's old notebooks were still in the chest beneath her bed."}
